comments.text:
Hashed environments keep a count in the hash table's truelength, but
maintain it inconsistently: R_HashSet() increments it for every new
binding (its search loop always ends at an empty chain, so its
"if (ISNULL(chain))" guard never fails), while a resize, attach() and
R_RestoreHashCount() (run after loading a saved environment) recount it
as the number of occupied chains.  So env.profile()$nchains, documented
as the number of non-empty chains, is wrong after any insert and
changes value across a serialization round trip, and the resize
threshold -- 85% of this count, in R_HashSizeCheck() -- compares
against neither an occupancy nor a load factor.

Reproducible example (R-devel, r90483):

  e <- new.env(hash = TRUE, size = 1000L)
  for (k in paste0("v", 1:500)) assign(k, k, envir = e)
  p <- env.profile(e)
  c(reported = p$nchains, actual = sum(p$counts > 0))
  ## observed: reported = 500 (the number of bindings), actual = 457
  ## expected: reported = 457
  e2 <- unserialize(serialize(e, NULL))
  env.profile(e2)$nchains
  ## observed: 457 -- the same table reports a different nchains after
  ## the round trip, because loading recounted occupied chains

Analysis:

All of this is in src/main/envir.c.  R_HashSet() effectively counts
bindings (the empty-chain guard at the end of its search loop is always
true), while R_HashResize(), R_HashFrame() and R_RestoreHashCount()
count occupied chains, and R_HashDelete() decrements only when a chain
becomes empty.  R_HashProfile() reports this mixture as nchains, and
R_HashSizeCheck() grows the table by 20% when the mixture exceeds 85%
of the size.

Proposed patch (also attached): the count, still stored in the table's
truelength, becomes the exact number of bindings (HASHCOUNT, formerly
HASHPRI), maintained by insert, delete, resize, attach() and the
load-time restore, so the resize threshold becomes a true load factor;
env.profile() counts the non-empty chains itself.  The growth policy is
otherwise unchanged: grow by 20% at more than 0.85 bindings per chain,
which is what the insert path already measured between resizes.  The
CHARSXP cache at the end of envir.c stores its own count of occupied
chains in the same slot; it keeps the old HASHPRI accessors and its
behavior is unchanged.  R Internals and ?environment are updated, and a
regression test checks the count against the contents after inserts,
removals and a serialization round trip.

